---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const asideLinks = [
  { label: 'Before you contact support', href: '#before-you-contact-support' },
  { label: 'Manufacturer support', href: '#manufacturer-support' },
  { label: 'Repair and data recovery', href: '#repair-and-data-recovery' },
  { label: 'Account recovery', href: '#account-recovery' },
  { label: 'Urgent safety issues', href: '#urgent-safety-issues' },
  { label: 'Avoiding support scams', href: '#avoiding-support-scams' },
];

const supportLinks = [
  { name: 'Microsoft support', href: 'https://support.microsoft.com/', note: 'Windows, Microsoft 365, Outlook, Xbox and account help.' },
  { name: 'Apple support', href: 'https://support.apple.com/', note: 'iPhone, iPad, Mac, Apple ID and Apple service.' },
  { name: 'Google support', href: 'https://support.google.com/', note: 'Android, Gmail, Google Account, Chrome and Nest.' },
  { name: 'Samsung support', href: 'https://www.samsung.com/us/support/', note: 'Galaxy phones, tablets, TVs and appliances.' },
  { name: 'HP support', href: 'https://support.hp.com/', note: 'Laptops, desktops and printers.' },
  { name: 'Dell support', href: 'https://www.dell.com/support/home/', note: 'Laptops, desktops, monitors and drivers.' },
  { name: 'Lenovo support', href: 'https://support.lenovo.com/', note: 'ThinkPad, IdeaPad, Legion and desktop systems.' },
  { name: 'Canon support', href: 'https://www.usa.canon.com/support', note: 'Printers, cameras and scanners.' },
  { name: 'Epson support', href: 'https://epson.com/Support/sl/s', note: 'Printers, scanners and projectors.' },
  { name: 'Brother support', href: 'https://support.brother.com/', note: 'Printers, scanners and label makers.' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Get additional help"
  metaTitle="Get Additional Help: Support, Repair and Recovery"
  description="Find the right kind of help when a troubleshooting guide is not enough: manufacturer support, repair, data recovery, account recovery and scam avoidance."
  canonical="/get-help/"
  eyebrow="Support options"
  intro="Some problems need official tools, a replacement part, or an account recovery process that only the provider can complete. This page explains where to go next and what to prepare."
  asideLinks={asideLinks}
  asideNote="FixProblemNow does not provide remote access, paid repairs, account recovery, or a live support line."
>
  <h2 id="before-you-contact-support">Before you contact support</h2>
  <p>
    A little preparation makes any support conversation shorter. Write down the device model,
    operating-system version, the exact error text, and what changed before the problem started.
    Note which steps you have already tried and what happened after each one. If the problem is
    intermittent, record roughly when it occurs and whether it affects one app, one network, or the
    whole device.
  </p>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Device model and serial number, if it is accessible.</li>
    <li>Operating-system version and the date of the last update.</li>
    <li>The exact error message, stop code, or on-screen warning.</li>
    <li>Whether the problem affects one app, one user account, or every user.</li>
    <li>What you changed immediately before the problem began.</li>
    <li>Which guides or fixes you have already tried, and the result.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout callout--warning">
    <p class="callout__label">Do not share more than necessary</p>
    <p>
      Never send a password, one-time verification code, recovery key, or full payment-card number
      to a support agent, forum, or email address. A legitimate support agent should not ask you to
      read out a code that appeared on your phone, and should not ask for remote access before
      explaining what they will do.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="manufacturer-support">Manufacturer support</h2>
  <p>
    Use the manufacturer’s own support site for warranty questions, official drivers, recovery
    images, firmware, diagnostics, and model-specific reset procedures. Third-party driver bundles
    and unofficial firmware can introduce malware or break the device’s warranty terms.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Support site</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical coverage</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {supportLinks.map((link) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={link.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                {link.name}
                <Icon name="external" size={12} />
              </a>
            </td>
            <td>{link.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    For internet outages, contact your internet provider rather than the router manufacturer once
    you have confirmed that the router has no internet connection and a restart has not helped.
    Have your account number and the router’s status-light behaviour ready.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repair-and-data-recovery">Repair and data recovery</h2>
  <p>
    Look for a repair provider that advertises the specific repair you need, provides a written
    estimate, and explains whether diagnostic fees apply. Ask whether the repair will affect your
    data and whether they will test the device with you before you leave. For a device containing
    important files, ask about data handling before authorising a repair that may erase storage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Data recovery is different from repair. A repair shop may replace a failed drive; a data
    recovery service specialises in reading a drive that no longer mounts. If a drive is clicking,
    grinding, or has been exposed to liquid, stop using it and seek specialist recovery rather than
    running repeated repair tools. Every additional write can reduce what is recoverable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="account-recovery">Account recovery</h2>
  <p>
    Account recovery must be handled by the company that controls the account. Use the official
    sign-in page and choose the “forgot password” or “cannot sign in” route. Do not use a link from
    an unsolicited email or message. For an account protected by two-factor authentication, use the
    provider’s documented recovery codes or account recovery process; no third-party service can
    legitimately bypass that protection.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a work or school account is locked, contact its administrator. They may be able to reset
    access without the public recovery flow, and they are the only party allowed to change some
    organisational security settings.
  </p>

  <h2 id="urgent-safety-issues">Urgent safety issues</h2>
  <div class="callout callout--important">
    <p class="callout__label">Stop and treat it as a safety issue</p>
    <p>
      Disconnect power if it is safe to do so and move away from a device that is smoking,
      sparking, producing a burning smell, or has a swollen battery. Do not open the device or
      continue charging it. For fire, smoke, or a burning smell that does not stop, contact your
      local emergency service. For a swollen battery, use a qualified repair or battery-disposal
      service rather than puncturing or pressing the battery.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Liquid damage is also a stopping point for DIY work. Power the device off, do not charge it, and
    do not use rice or heat to dry it. Seek service that can clean and inspect the internals.
  </p>

  <h2 id="avoiding-support-scams">Avoiding support scams</h2>
  <ul class="icon-list">
    <li>
      <Icon name="alert" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>Unexpected calls or pop-ups.</strong>
        <span>A browser pop-up or phone call claiming your device is infected is not a diagnosis. Close the window or end the call.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <Icon name="alert" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>Remote access before explanation.</strong>
        <span>A legitimate support process should tell you what it needs and why. Refuse remote access you did not initiate.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <Icon name="alert" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>Payment by gift card or transfer.</strong>
        <span>Requests for gift cards, cryptocurrency, or a bank transfer to an individual are common scam patterns.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <Icon name="alert" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>Search-advertised phone numbers.</strong>
        <span>Use the support site you typed yourself, not a phone number from an advertisement or an unsolicited search result.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you have already given someone remote access, disconnect the network connection, change
    important passwords from a different device, and check for unfamiliar remote-access software.
    Report the incident to your bank if payment details were shared.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
